# Portfolio Dashboard - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
Enterprise fullstack application with Next.js frontend, FastAPI backend, and PostgreSQL database.

## Technology Stack
- **Frontend**: Next.js 15, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, React 19
- **Backend**: FastAPI, Python 3.x, SQLAlchemy, Alembic
- **Database**: PostgreSQL 15+
- **Deployment**: Docker Compose (dev), AWS (production)

## General Guidelines
- Write clean, readable, and maintainable code
- Follow DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Write self-documenting code with clear comments
- Follow consistent formatting and style guidelines
- Never commit secrets to version control
- Use environment variables for sensitive configuration

## Frontend Standards (TypeScript/React)
- Use TypeScript interfaces for type definitions
- Prefer `interface` over `type` for object shapes
- Use proper typing for props, state, and API responses
- Avoid `any` type; use `unknown` when type is truly unknown
- Use path aliases (`@/*`) for imports
- Use functional components with hooks
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Use React.memo() for performance optimization when needed
- Follow single responsibility principle for components
- Use custom hooks for reusable logic

## Styling Guidelines
- Use Tailwind CSS utility classes
- Follow the design system defined in tailwind.config.js
- Use CSS custom properties for theming
- Keep styles consistent across components
- Use responsive design principles

## Backend Standards (Python/FastAPI)
- Follow PEP 8 style guidelines
- Use type hints for function parameters and return values
- Use docstrings for functions and classes
- Follow single responsibility principle
- Use dependency injection for better testability
- Use Pydantic models for request/response validation
- Implement proper error handling with HTTPException
- Document APIs with OpenAPI/Swagger
- Follow RESTful API design principles

## Database Standards
- Use SQLAlchemy 2.0+ syntax
- Define proper relationships between models
- Use Alembic for database migrations
- Follow naming conventions (snake_case)
- Implement proper indexing for performance

## Security Standards
- Use proper password hashing (bcrypt)
- Implement JWT token authentication
- Use HTTPS in production
- Validate and sanitize all inputs
- Follow OWASP security guidelines

## Testing Standards
- Write unit tests for components and utilities
- Use React Testing Library for component testing
- Use Jest for frontend testing
- Use pytest for backend testing
- Aim for meaningful test coverage
- Test user interactions and edge cases
- Mock external dependencies appropriately
- Write tests before or alongside code
- Use descriptive test names
- Test both happy path and edge cases

## Code Organization
- Keep related functionality together
- Use clear directory structure
- Separate concerns (models, services, controllers)
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Group related files in appropriate directories
- Use absolute imports when possible
- Group imports: standard library, third-party, local
- Use path aliases for cleaner imports
- Avoid circular dependencies

## Documentation Standards
- Write clear README files
- Document API endpoints
- Use meaningful commit messages
- Document complex business logic
- Keep documentation up to date
- Update docs as you work

## Performance Standards
- Optimize database queries
- Use proper caching strategies
- Implement lazy loading where appropriate
- Monitor and profile performance
- Use async/await for I/O operations
- Use Next.js Image component for optimized images
- Implement proper loading states
- Use dynamic imports for code splitting

## Error Handling
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Use meaningful error messages
- Log errors appropriately
- Handle edge cases gracefully
- Provide user-friendly error feedback

## Development Workflow
- Use Docker Compose for development environment
- Use Makefile commands for common tasks
- Use meaningful commit messages
- Follow conventional commit format when possible
- Create feature branches for new development
- Use pull requests for code review
- Run tests before committing
- Use linters (ESLint, Black, flake8)

## Enterprise Features
- Implement audit logging
- Follow compliance reporting requirements
- Maintain data governance standards
- Use role-based access control
- Implement SSO integration where applicable

## File Structure Expectations
```
portfolio-dashboard/
├── frontend/                 # Next.js frontend
│   ├── src/
│   │   ├── app/             # App Router pages
│   │   ├── hooks/           # Custom hooks
│   │   └── lib/             # Utilities
├── backend/                  # FastAPI backend
│   ├── app/
│   │   ├── api/             # API routes
│   │   ├── models/          # SQLAlchemy models
│   │   ├── schemas/         # Pydantic schemas
│   │   └── services/        # Business logic
├── database/                 # Database files
├── docs/                    # Documentation
└── docker-compose.yml       # Development environment
```

## Communication Style
- Be direct and concise
- Provide specific examples
- Explain the "why" behind recommendations
- Focus on practical solutions
- Maintain professional tone
- Ask clarifying questions when needed

## Code Review Focus Areas
- Functionality and correctness
- Security vulnerabilities
- Performance implications
- Error handling completeness
- Test coverage adequacy
- Documentation updates
- Code style consistency

## API Development Patterns
- Use RESTful API design principles
- Implement proper HTTP status codes (200, 201, 400, 401, 404, 500)
- Use Pydantic models for request/response validation
- Implement rate limiting for public endpoints
- Use OpenAPI/Swagger documentation
- Follow REST resource naming conventions (/api/v1/users, /api/v1/users/{id})
- Implement proper pagination for list endpoints
- Use query parameters for filtering and sorting

## Database Patterns
- Use repository pattern for data access
- Implement proper indexing for frequently queried fields
- Use database transactions for multi-table operations
- Implement soft deletes instead of hard deletes
- Use database migrations for schema changes
- Implement proper foreign key constraints
- Use database connection pooling
- Implement database query optimization

## Authentication & Authorization Patterns
- Use JWT tokens for stateless authentication
- Implement refresh token mechanism
- Use bcrypt for password hashing
- Implement role-based access control (RBAC)
- Use middleware for authentication checks
- Implement proper session management
- Use HTTPS for all authentication endpoints
- Implement proper logout functionality

## Error Handling Patterns
- Use specific exception types instead of generic Exception
- Implement proper error logging with context
- Use structured error responses with error codes
- Implement global error handlers
- Use try-catch blocks for external API calls
- Implement proper validation error handling
- Use custom exception classes for business logic errors
- Implement proper error monitoring and alerting

